WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.781
A car drives a loop through Karlsruhe.

00:00:02.781 --> 00:00:05.444
Its IMU logs a hundred times a second.

00:00:05.444 --> 00:00:07.775
Noisy position fixes arrive once a second.

00:00:08.155 --> 00:00:10.041
For thirty seconds, the fixes stop.

00:00:10.041 --> 00:00:13.812
Every filter drifts about forty metres, and the good ones know it.

00:00:16.377 --> 00:00:17.231
Hi, I'm Rudolf.

00:00:17.231 --> 00:00:20.646
Four filters, one real drive, and a test that tells you which

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:21.784
of them is lying.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.196
Every Kalman filter runs the same update.

00:00:27.616 --> 00:00:28.792
Start from the prediction.

00:00:30.857 --> 00:00:34.218
Take the innovation, the part of the measurement you did not expect.

00:00:34.682 --> 00:00:36.151
And move by the gain.

00:00:36.151 --> 00:00:39.969
It weighs how unsure the prediction is against how unsure the innovation is.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:42.368
Rotations break it.

00:00:42.368 --> 00:00:46.346
A quaternion lives on a sphere, and adding a correction walks it off.

00:00:46.726 --> 00:00:50.134
The error-state filter keeps the error in the tangent line at its

00:00:50.134 --> 00:00:51.838
estimate, then wraps it back on.

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:56.269
Here a heading is a point on a circle.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:57.789
The filter predicts one direction.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:00.829
The sensor says the body is seventy degrees further round.

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:04.244
The classic EKF adds its correction in a straight line.

00:01:04.244 --> 00:01:08.436
It leaves the circle, so the code divides by the length and lands short.

00:01:08.856 --> 00:01:13.147
The error-state filter solves for an angle and wraps it along the circle.

00:01:13.147 --> 00:01:15.127
It stays a rotation by construction.

00:01:15.547 --> 00:01:19.188
The iterated filter relinearises at its new guess and solves again.

00:01:19.188 --> 00:01:22.167
Five passes later it is almost at the truth.

00:01:23.992 --> 00:01:27.120
That iterated update is the loop inside FAST-LIO.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.640
It runs on every LiDAR scan, until it converges.

00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:36.145
On the real drive, with ranges to beacons and a start thirty

00:01:36.145 --> 00:01:39.745
metres off, one linear step leaves the EKF eighteen metres out.

00:01:39.745 --> 00:01:41.709
Iterating gets within about a metre.

00:01:43.983 --> 00:01:47.608
To check a filter, weigh its errors by its own covariance.

00:01:47.608 --> 00:01:51.563
That is NEES, and a consistent filter averages the number of states.

00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:56.258
Cut the process noise a hundredfold and NEES jumps past one hundred sixty.

00:01:56.258 --> 00:01:59.876
The filter is almost twice as wrong, and sure of itself.

00:02:01.608 --> 00:02:04.782
A filter folds the past into one Gaussian and forgets it.

00:02:05.448 --> 00:02:09.319
Run it backwards too, the factor graph's trick, and the error more than halves.

00:02:09.319 --> 00:02:11.254
When the answer can wait, look back.

00:02:12.725 --> 00:02:14.721
Keep rotations in an error state.

00:02:14.721 --> 00:02:16.384
Iterate when the measurement bends.

00:02:16.384 --> 00:02:19.045
And log the innovation test from day one.

00:02:20.442 --> 00:02:24.360
The gain weighs two spreads, error states keep rotations honest, and the

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.972
chi-square tests tell you when a filter lies.

00:02:27.392 --> 00:02:29.474
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:29.474 --> 00:02:30.069
I'm Rudolf.

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:30.366
Bye!
